## Duties during your week

- **Watch feedback for 48 hours after a release**: user reports and issues passed on by support, error monitoring, whether key usage metrics dropped noticeably, whether the new version starts or deploys normally.
- **When a release is bad**: stop the damage first, then fix. {{#if stagedRollout}}Pause the staged rollout (set it to 0%) first{{else}}Pull or roll back the release first (revert the {{ pr }} that caused it, or ship the last good version again){{/if}}, then find the change's author and fix it together. The fix goes through normal review at a risk tier of at least medium.
{{#if queue.self}}
- **The merge queue is stuck**: read the runs of `.github/workflows/{{ policy.names.queueWorkflow }}` and the comments on ejected {{ pr }}s.{{#if ci.selfHosted}} When a self-hosted runner is offline, the checks that run on it stay queued: wake the machine or restart the runner.{{else}}{{#if ci.releaseGate.selfHosted}} When a self-hosted runner is offline, the checks that run on it stay queued: wake the machine or restart the runner.{{/if}}{{/if}}
{{else}}
- **The merge queue is stuck**: look at the repository's merge queue page and the required checks of the {{ pr }} at the front.{{#if ci.selfHosted}} When a self-hosted runner is offline, the checks that run on it stay queued: wake the machine or restart the runner.{{else}}{{#if ci.releaseGate.selfHosted}} When a self-hosted runner is offline, the checks that run on it stay queued: wake the machine or restart the runner.{{/if}}{{/if}}
{{/if}}
{{#if loops.metrics}}
- **Read the weekly metrics every Monday**: the issue labeled `{{ policy.names.metricsLabel }}` shows human review time, reverts and fixes, {{ ai }}'s false-positive rate{{#if queue.self}} and the number of waivers{{/if}}. Bring anything unusual to the weekly meeting.
{{/if}}
{{#if loops.sweep}}
- **Triage nightly sweep issues**: labeled `{{ policy.names.sweepLabel }}`. Check whether each one is real: if so, open a {{ pr }} or hand it to someone who knows that code; if not, close it with the reason.
{{/if}}
{{#if loops.eval}}
- **Check the review eval results**: the issue labeled `{{ policy.names.evalLabel }}`. If the hit rate dropped, look first at what changed in the review instructions recently.
{{/if}}

## After an incident

Write the incident review with `docs/incident-review-template.md` and put it in the incident's issue. The review asks which layer of the system should have caught the problem, never who is to blame; "you land it, you own it" exists because the author knows the context best.
